## 2026-07-29 (session 17)
Attempted: task 1.9 — time/clock formatting, the last task of Phase 1.
Done: 1.9, flipped in MIGRATION_PLAN.md (closes Phase 1 — Foundations).
  New `noctalia-core::time` (crates/noctalia-core/src/time/{mod,strftime,
  locale,zone,relative,service}.rs), a port of `src/time/time_format.{cpp,h}`
  and `src/time/time_service.{cpp,h}`. `time_poll_source.h` has no port,
  same reasoning as task 1.2's `FileWatchPollSource`: pure `PollSource`
  glue with zero independent logic (verified by reading it), belongs with
  calloop main-loop wiring whenever that's assembled.
  Central design finding, made by prototyping directly against the real
  `jiff` crate in a throwaway `/tmp` project inside the dev shell (not
  guessed from memory): the C++ genuinely runs two independent formatting
  engines — a `strftime`-compatible path (`formatStrftimeCompat`/
  `formatStrftimeRaw`) that every real call site in `src/` reaches (grepped
  every caller; all pass a format string containing `%`), and a
  `std::vformat` chrono-formatter fallback that's unreachable by any of
  them. This port implements only the first engine, and builds it on
  `libc::strftime` directly (matching `core::log`'s existing raw-libc
  idiom) rather than `jiff`'s own `strtime` formatter: verified (by reading
  jiff 0.2.35's `fmt::strtime` source) that jiff's weekday/month names are
  hardcoded English with no locale support, whereas real call sites depend
  on locale-aware names — `widget_config.cpp:96`'s default clock format is
  `"{:%a %d %b}"`, seeded into every profile's `date` widget. Using jiff
  there would have silently anglicized clocks under non-English `LC_TIME`.
  `jiff` ends up used in exactly one place, `zone.rs`'s `format_timezone_time`/
  `is_valid_timezone` (architecture decision 5's actual justification: a
  real IANA timezone database with correct historical DST rules) —
  everything else in the module only ever needs system-local time
  (`libc::localtime_r`, already used elsewhere in this crate) or pure
  calendar math (`locale.rs`'s anchor-weekday computation, via
  `jiff::civil::Date` with no timezone/tzdb involved at all). Confirmed
  empirically that jiff prefers the system's `/usr/share/zoneinfo` (this
  host's real Nix-provided tzdata) over its own bundled copy by default on
  Linux, matching C++'s `std::chrono` zone source exactly — no extra
  feature flags needed.
  `format_timezone_time` computes offset/abbreviation/DST/wall-clock fields
  from jiff's `Zoned`/`TimeZone::to_offset_info`, then populates a
  `libc::tm` and renders through the same `libc::strftime`-based engine as
  everything else (mirroring the C++'s own approach of manually populating
  a `std::tm` from `year_month_day`/`hh_mm_ss`/zone info before calling its
  own strftime bridge) — verified end-to-end against Pacific/Kiritimati
  (fixed +14:00, no DST) producing byte-identical `"+1400|+14"` for `%z|%Z`
  before writing the real module.
  `_NL_TIME_FIRST_WEEKDAY`/`_NL_TIME_WEEK_1STDAY` (glibc-only
  `<langinfo.h>` extension items `localeFirstDayOfWeek` needs) aren't
  exposed by the `libc` crate (confirmed by grepping its source) — hardcoded
  as `131176`/`131174` after hand-verifying against both a throwaway C
  program linked against this host's real glibc AND (independently, by the
  pre-done reviewer below) the actual installed glibc-dev header.
  Two more divergences, both recorded in module doc comments: `format_file_time`
  takes `Option<SystemTime>` instead of C++'s `file_time_type` +
  sentinel-equality check (also drops the C++'s clock-domain conversion
  dance entirely, since `SystemTime` already is wall-clock time);
  `format_duration` takes `std::time::Duration` (unsigned) instead of
  signed `chrono::seconds`, matching every real call site already being
  non-negative.
  Tests: `tests/time_format_test.rs` ports every assertion in
  `tests/time_format_test.cpp` verbatim (epoch-token substitution,
